export const SKILL_BODIES: Record<string, string> = {
  "opencode-app-e2e-tests": "# App e2e tests\n\nRead `packages/app/e2e/AGENTS.md` and the Tests section of `packages/app/AGENTS.md` first. They define what to test, which locators to use, and how to assert. `packages/app/README.md` (E2E Testing) documents run modes and env vars. This skill covers what makes these tests slow or flaky in CI, which runs the suite as two shards per OS on 4-vCPU runners (`.github/workflows/check-test.yml:217-240`). Most races below were found on Windows.\n\nAll paths below are relative to `packages/app` unless they start with `packages/`, `services/` or `.github/`.\n\n## What a test runs against\n\n- **No opencode backend.** Each shard leg runs `bun --cwd packages/app test:e2e:built --shard=<n>/2` (`check-test.yml:287-292`). It builds once and starts one Bun static server, `e2e/utils/serve.ts`, on `127.0.0.1:${PLAYWRIGHT_PORT ?? 3000}`. All 5 workers share it (`playwright.config.ts:23-27`).\n  - `serve.ts` keeps vite preview's SPA fallback rules. Do not move built e2e back to vite preview. On case-insensitive file systems it synchronously reads the whole `dist/_assets` directory (about 3,100 files) for every asset request, so every worker's page loads wait in line. The `e2e/performance` configs still use it to keep their baselines comparable.\n- **The API is an in-memory mock per page,** running in the test worker. `mockOpenCodeServer` (`e2e/utils/mock-server.ts:202`):\n  - answers `page.route(\"**/api/**\")` with an Effect HttpApi handler;\n  - fakes `/api/event` SSE with an init-script `fetch` override;\n  - serves PTY sockets through `routeWebSocket`.\n- **Unmocked routes fail the test** with a 501 and `Unmocked API request` (`mock-server.ts:403-411`). Add the route to `mock-server.ts` once instead of routing around it in a spec.\n- **Build pages with the helpers:**\n  - `mockWorkspace`, `openSession`, `openDraft`, `openWorktreeDraft`, `mockRemoteServer`, `openSettings` (`e2e/utils/workspace.ts`);\n  - `setupTimeline` (`e2e/utils/timeline.ts:209`) for streamed timelines, `mockServers` (`mock-server.ts:495`) for several origins;\n  - `seed` (`e2e/utils/app.ts:61`) for storage, `holdRoute` (`app.ts:207`) to hold requests until you release them.\n- **Deliver events** with the mock's `push`, which buffers until the app connects, or with `installSseTransport` (`e2e/utils/sse-transport.ts:60`), whose `send` resolves on delivery. After `page.reload()`, await `transport.waitForConnection()` before sending (`e2e/regression/timeline-stream.spec.ts:1700-1702`).\n- **Windows-only failures are races, not Windows code paths.** Mock directories are `C:/OpenCode/<name>` on every OS (`workspace.ts:33`), and nothing touches the disk. Lazy chunks, stylesheets and responses finish out of order more often on the Windows runners.\n\n## Isolation\n\n- **No shared state between tests.** Each test gets a fresh context, mock handler and init scripts. Outside `e2e/service-worker`, which runs under its own config and shares builds through a worker fixture (`e2e/service-worker/cache.spec.ts:21`, `:74`), app e2e has no `beforeAll`, serial mode, `test.extend` or worker fixtures. Keep it that way so `-g` and `PLAYWRIGHT_FULLY_PARALLEL=1` keep working.\n- **Handlers mutate the objects you pass.** Creating a session pushes into `sessions` (`mock-server.ts:839`). Renaming writes `session.title` (`:1340`), and VCS init writes `project.vcs` (`:1019`).\n  - Call `session()`/`project()` inside the test.\n  - `mockWorkspace` and `openSession` copy what you pass (`workspace.ts:36-48`).\n  - `mockOpenCodeServer` and `mockServers` use your objects as they are. Module-level fixtures like `multi-server.spec.ts:10-19` are therefore shared by every test in the file.\n- **Timeline builders keep state per test attempt** (`timeline.ts:165-198`). Call stateful builders such as `assistantMessage` or `partUpdated` inside the test. Only pure ones such as `toolPart` belong at module scope.\n- **Seed before the first `goto`.** `seed` writes storage once per tab behind a `sessionStorage` marker, so reloads keep what the app stored (`app.ts:59-90`). It cannot reset state mid-test.\n\n## Keep it fast\n\n- **Navigate once.** Every `goto` or `reload` is a cold load of the production bundle: about 150 requests per session open, about 125 of them static files. Each test has a new context, and any `page.route` disables the HTTP cache. Reach later states through the UI or through pushed events. Reload only when surviving a reload is the contract.\n- **Service workers are already blocked suite-wide** (`serviceWorkers: \"block\"`, `playwright.config.ts:66-67`). Do not add a per-file block; the 11 in `e2e/regression` are redundant.\n  - Production registers `/sw.js` on load (`src/entry.tsx:54-55`) and precaches about 1,600 files (`globPatterns: [\"**/*\"]`, `vite.pwa.ts:34`). `e2e/service-worker/` covers it under its own config. The main config ignores that directory (`playwright.config.ts:33-36`), and CI runs it after shard 2 (`check-test.yml:294-298`).\n  - Opt back in with `test.use({ serviceWorkers: \"allow\" })` only when the worker is the contract. It never controls the first load (`clientsClaim: false`, `vite.pwa.ts:26-28`). After a reload it can control the page, and page routes may then miss its requests.\n  - Every built-mode page load logs Playwright's block warning. Console collectors must ignore it, as `captureConsoleWarnings` does (`e2e/utils/command-palette.ts:15-26`).\n- **Match new routes with a RegExp or a query-aware glob, not a function.**\n  - A single function matcher makes Playwright intercept `**/*` for the page. Every static asset then round-trips to the test worker (250 continued requests in one Windows test).\n  - Existing `holdRoute` calls such as `review.spec.ts:51` and `mockMissingLocation` (`mock-server.ts:480`) still use functions and pay this. Converting one route gains nothing while another function route is registered on the page.\n  - Globs match the whole URL, so `**/api/vcs/init` misses `/api/vcs/init?location...`. Write `/\\/api\\/vcs\\/init\\?/` or `\"**/api/session?*\"` (`command-palette.spec.ts:10`, `:28`).\n- **Use the stress fixture only when scale is the contract.** `fixture`, `mockStressTimeline` and `installStressSessionTabs` (`e2e/utils/session-fixture.ts:336`, `:436`, `:448`) serve a long timeline with 300-line diffs.\n  - Its cold mount reveals rows only after worker-rendered markdown arrives. Mounting one of its diffs blocked the renderer for 4-11 s on CI.\n  - The 14 regression files that use it held about half of the e2e flakes CI found.\n  - Prefer `openSession` or `setupTimeline` with the few messages the test needs.\n- **Assert inside loops only when each step is the contract.** A web-first assertion that fails its first check retries after 100, 250, 500, then 1,000 ms. The 10-step loop at `mobile-timeline-scroll.spec.ts:688-693` takes about 8 s, mostly backoff.\n- **Real-time waits cost their full length on every run and retry** (`settings-wsl.spec.ts:268-276` waits out a 10 s health check). Prefer `page.clock`:\n  - install it before the `goto` or `reload` whose timers you need (`timeline-stream.spec.ts:1700`);\n  - keep fast-forwards under the client's 45 s event-stream idle timeout (`packages/client/src/solid/connection.ts:35`).\n- **Leave benchmark pacing out of regression tests.** The `sendAll` delays and `messageDelay` exist for benchmarks (`timeline.ts:306`, `mock-server.ts:82`). Hold message pages with `beforeMessagesResponse` instead.\n\n## Wait for state, not time: races CI found\n\nMost of these rarely reproduce on an idle local machine.\n\n- **First mount happens in stages.** `openSession` and `expectSessionTitle` return once the composer is editable and a title is visible. Later the loading header's `<h1>` is replaced, and a cold timeline keeps its rows hidden while it scrolls itself to the bottom. Before you measure or hover in the session view, wait for something that only the final mount renders, such as the last `[data-timeline-row=\"UserMessage\"]` or the row you click next.\n- **Hovering during a scroll loses the tooltip.** A tooltip closes when its trigger scrolls, and Playwright's mouse never moves again. `session-summary.spec.ts:21-26` waits for the last row before it hovers; without that wait it needed a retry in 6 of 36 Windows runs.\n- **One-shot measurements can hit a detached node.** `locator.evaluate` resolves the element, then evaluates in a second round trip. If the node is replaced in between, `getBoundingClientRect` returns an all-zero rect and `boundingBox` returns `null`. The subagent title-anchor test failed with \"Expected: 0\" in 11 runs until it waited for a timeline-only button (`subagent-child-navigation.spec.ts:60-64`). Take reference measurements once the final structure is in place. Polled reads re-resolve and are safe.\n- **`goto` returns late, so a modal may already be open.**\n  - `goto` waits for `load`, which can fire after the app is already interactive.\n  - An open dialog sets `aria-hidden` on the app root, which hides everything behind it from role locators.\n  - What's New opens 500 ms after the changelog response, and it raced the Settings check in 11 of 36 Windows runs. Hold the request that opens a modal until readiness is asserted, as `startUpgraded` does (`whats-new.spec.ts:227-234`).\n- **`pauseAt(new Date())` right after `install()` can throw** \"Cannot fast-forward to the past\", because the installed clock starts slightly ahead of the runner's clock. Install at an explicit time and pause at an offset from it (`tabs.spec.ts:443-445`).\n- **A shortcut that does nothing is usually a product guard, not timing.** The global handler ignores keys while `dialog.active` (`src/shell/commands/command.tsx:489`). `active` is the top dialog that has not started closing (`packages/ui/src/context/dialog.tsx:52`, `:310-311`). A dialog that has started closing does not block keys during its 100 ms exit (`dialog.tsx:87`), but any open dialog does, including one you did not expect. Check that guard before you add a wait.\n- **Shortcut tests fail on local macOS; that is not a regression.**\n  - The Desktop Chrome profile reports a Windows UA and `navigator.platform = \"Win32\"`, so the app (`IS_MAC`, `command.tsx:14`) expects Ctrl. On macOS, `ControlOrMeta` sends Meta.\n  - Every test that opens the palette through `openCommandPalette` (`e2e/utils/command-palette.ts:50`) fails locally and passes in CI, as do `tabs.spec.ts:835` and `timeline-cache.spec.ts:163`. Verify on Linux or in CI, or press `Control` in a throwaway copy. Do not edit tests for macOS.\n  - A new test can pick the modifier from the page, as `session-summary.spec.ts:29-30` does.\n- **Font probes mislead.** `document.fonts.check()` can stay false after the font has loaded: when a lazy `@layer` stylesheet activates late, Chromium rebuilds font faces as unloaded. The code-font test failed outright in 9 of 36 Windows runs that way. Polling a probe that lays out text can make itself true. Await `document.fonts.ready`, lay out new text, and then check once (`timeline-cache.spec.ts:281-293`).\n- **Use the harness's signals.** `holdRoute(...).arrived` resolves when the request reaches the route, and `transport.send` resolves after delivery. `setupTimeline(...).waitForPart` resolves once a part renders (`timeline.ts:328`).\n- **Decide which side is wrong.** Three of eight investigated e2e flakes were product bugs, each since fixed in the product:\n  - a stale scroll offset restored when a scroll settles (`src/session/timeline/observe-element-offset.ts`);\n  - a video double-click that also entered native fullscreen (`packages/gui-plugins/src/file/artifact-view.tsx:612`);\n  - shortcuts dropped while a dialog closed.\n\n  If a user could hit the interleaving, fix the product and keep the assertion.\n\n## Scheduling\n\n- **Two projects split the suite** (`playwright.config.ts:69-86`). `chromium-touch-scroll` runs only `mobile-timeline-scroll.spec.ts`, which the `touchScroll` regex (`:29`) matches, with `fullyParallel: true`.\n  - Projects queue in declaration order, so its 37 tests start after every `chromium` file and spread across all workers. As one serial group, the file took 3-5 minutes in CI.\n  - A rename that the regex misses quietly puts the file back in `chromium` as that serial group.\n  - `--project chromium`, UI mode and the VS Code extension's default project skip the file.\n- **In `chromium`, files are the scheduling unit.** `fullyParallel` is off unless `PLAYWRIGHT_FULLY_PARALLEL=1` (`:42`), so a file's tests run in order on one worker. A long file sets a floor and leaves an idle tail. New regressions go into the area's keeper file (`e2e/AGENTS.md`), so keep each case cheap.\n- **Shards split whole files by test count, not duration** (`check-test.yml:229-232`). The touch project queues last, so its tests always fall in shard 2. To reproduce a leg's schedule, pass the same `--shard`.\n- **Worker-scoped options fork a worker.** `launchOptions`, `channel`, `headless`, `video`, `trace`, `screenshot` and `defaultBrowserType` give a file its own worker hash.\n  - Playwright groups a project's files by worker hash in order of first appearance, so such a file runs after every default file in its project.\n  - Use test-scoped options instead: `viewport`, `deviceScaleFactor`, `hasTouch`, `isMobile`, `userAgent`, `colorScheme`, `serviceWorkers`, `locale`.\n  - Use `launchOptions` only when a Chromium flag is the contract, with a comment (`patch-border.spec.ts:5-9`).\n- **Never spread `devices[...]` into `test.use`.** iPhone entries set `defaultBrowserType: \"webkit\"`, which forks a worker and needs a browser that CI never installs (`check-test.yml:282-285`). Copy the fields you need, as `mobile-timeline-scroll.spec.ts:23-28` does.\n- **Retries are not free.** CI retries twice (`playwright.config.ts:44`). Each failure restarts the worker's browser and costs 10-30 s of cleanup on Windows. The first retry then reruns with a trace and a video (`:62-65`).\n- **Timeouts are not a fix.** The limits are 60 s per test and 10 s per assertion (`playwright.config.ts:38-41`). `APP_READY_TIMEOUT` (30 s, `e2e/utils/waits.ts:4`) covers only first readiness.\n  - When one wait is genuinely long, scope the timeout to that assertion and say what takes the time. `settings-wsl.spec.ts:268-276` does this with `{ timeout: 20_000 }` for a 10 s health check.\n  - `test.setTimeout` does not lengthen assertions. `timeline-history.spec.ts:73` sets 240 s without a comment and still fails at the 10 s poll at `:127`.\n\n## Before you push\n\nRun from `packages/app`:\n\n- [ ] Run in built mode, as CI does: `bun run test:e2e:built -- e2e/regression/<file>.spec.ts`. Dev mode serves unbundled modules, with the API on another origin (`playwright.config.ts:19-21`).\n- [ ] Use a free port when another worktree or agent may hold 3000: `PLAYWRIGHT_PORT=4310 bun run test:e2e:built -- ...`. Built runs never reuse a server, and `serve.ts` fails on a busy port. Stay below 49152 on Windows: it reserves ports from there up and hands them out for outgoing connections (`services/www/src/docs/content/cli/web.mdx:43`, `packages/client/test/fixture/service-fixture.ts:108-109`).\n- [ ] To repeat without rebuilding:\n  - build once with `VITE_OPENCODE_TEST_FIXTURES=1 bun run build`;\n  - keep `bun e2e/utils/serve.ts --host 127.0.0.1 --port 4310` running;\n  - run `PLAYWRIGHT_BUILD=1 PLAYWRIGHT_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4310 bun run test:e2e -- ...`.\n- [ ] Repeat new or changed tests at CI concurrency without retries: `--repeat-each=10 --workers=5 --retries=0 -g \"<title>\"`. Run the file once with `PLAYWRIGHT_FULLY_PARALLEL=1` to prove that order does not matter.\n- [ ] Rehearse Windows timing by delaying assets, not only the CPU. CPU throttling at 4x and 6x did not reproduce the tooltip or title-anchor races, but delayed responses did.\n  - In a throwaway copy, hold the suspect lazy chunk, stylesheet or API response with `holdRoute`, and release it 0.6-1.5 s after `arrived`.\n  - Route worker scripts with `page.context().route`.\n  - A race fix must fail before the fix and pass after it (`packages/app/AGENTS.md`, Tests).\n- [ ] Check the cost on a noisy machine. Other agents add load, and local A/Bs at a load average of 20-35 were inconclusive.\n  - Compare the medians of interleaved before/after runs at `--workers=5`, or the step durations in the report (`bun run test:e2e:report`).\n  - Count every `goto` and `reload` you added.\n- [ ] `bun run typecheck:e2e` reports 4 existing errors: `timeline-stream.spec.ts:1822`, `:1825`, and two `e2e/performance` probes. Add none. If you changed `playwright.config.ts`, update `test-browser/playwright-config.test.ts`.\n\n## When CI flakes\n\n- **Find it.** e2e runs on pull requests that affect `@opencode/app` or `@opencode/stories`, including their workspace dependencies, and on every manual dispatch (`.github/workflows/check-test.yml:50-62`). It never runs on dev (`:28`).\n  - Each OS has the legs `app-1` and `app-2` (the shards), `session-ui-components` and `app-components`.\n  - The required `e2e (linux)` and `e2e (windows)` checks only aggregate those legs (`:329-352`).\n  - A pass on retry leaves a leg green. Check each Windows shard report's Flaky list, and make sure your new tests are not on it.\n- **Download:** `gh run download <run-id> -n playwright-windows-app-2-1 -D <dir>`. Artifacts are named `playwright-<os>-<leg>-<run attempt>` (`check-test.yml:314-327`). App e2e results are in `<dir>/app/e2e/`.\n- **In CI, the failing first attempt has no trace and no video** (`playwright.config.ts:62-65`).\n  - `test-results/<test>-<project>/` holds `error-context.md` (the aria snapshot at the failure) and `test-failed-1.png`.\n  - `<test>-<project>-retry1/` holds `trace.zip` and `video.webm` whether the retry passed or failed, plus failure files only if it failed. A second retry records neither.\n- **Read `error-context.md` first.** An open dialog or an element with no accessible name explains most \"element(s) not found\" errors.\n- **Get timings from the retry trace:** `bunx playwright show-trace <dir>/app/e2e/test-results/<test>-<project>-retry1/trace.zip`. To see which side won, compare when a screencast frame changed with when the response landed.\n- **Get the transient state from a failing video,** sampled frame by frame (`ffmpeg -i video.webm -vf fps=4 frame-%03d.png`). That is how the tooltip race showed the tooltip closing in the same frame the timeline rows appeared.\n  - In CI, retry1's video shows the failure only if the retry failed too.\n  - Locally, a failing test keeps its video (`retain-on-failure`), so you can record the race with `--repeat-each` and delayed assets.\n- **Open the report:** `bunx playwright show-report <dir>/app/e2e/playwright-report`. It shows step durations for every attempt, including passing ones.",
  "opencode-component-tests": "# Component tests\n\nEach package suite starts one story server, a plain Vite dev server for `packages/stories`, and every worker shares it (`packages/stories/playwright/config.ts:17-26`; CI uses 2 workers, `config.ts:15`). With `fullyParallel` (`config.ts:12`), the tests of one file run on several workers in any order. There is no backend: server, SDK and sync modules are in-page mocks. Every test gets a fresh context, and `mount` is a full page navigation to the frame (`packages/stories/playwright/story.ts:37`). Slow or flaky component tests in CI have come from five sources: the shared server's dependency optimizer, module resolution that depended on the install layout, a cold first story, a product race (the mobile drawer), or an action that ran before the UI reached the state it needed.\n\nRead these first rather than repeating them:\n\n- `packages/stories/playwright/README.md` covers layout, run commands and \"Adding a test\".\n- `packages/app/AGENTS.md` \"Tests\" decides whether a test should exist, when theme or direction cases are allowed, and that a regression test must fail before the fix.\n- `packages/app/e2e/AGENTS.md` \"Test Hygiene\" applies here unchanged.\n\n## Stories and `mount`\n\n- **Location.** Put the story next to the production component under `src/` of `ui`, `session-ui`, `app` or `gui-plugins`. The story server indexes `*.stories.tsx` files in those four trees (`packages/stories/vite.config.ts`, `src/index-plugin.ts`), reading each file's `export default { title, id }` and its `export const` stories from source.\n- **Story ID.** It is the meta `id` if one is set (`packages/session-ui/src/components/session-review.stories.tsx:25`). Otherwise it is the kebab-cased `title`, then `--`, then the kebab-cased export. Check it at `http://127.0.0.1:6006/?id=<id>` on a running story server (`bun --cwd packages/stories dev`). A wrong ID renders nothing (the page throws `No story has the id …`), so `mount` waits out its full 30 s root wait (`story.ts:39`) and then fails.\n- **URL args.** `mount(id, { args, globals })` writes them into the URL as `key:value` pairs (`story.ts:20-36`). The frame reads every one: a value becomes a boolean or number when the story's own default for that key is one, `!true`/`!false`/`!null`/`!undefined` keep Storybook's meaning, and anything else stays text.\n  - Give every arg a typed default. With `args: { split: false }` (`session-review.stories.tsx:129`), `split:false` arrives as a boolean.\n  - A value containing `;` would split the pair list, so pick such values inside the story, from a `scenario`-style arg.\n- **Globals** are `theme` (default `light`), `direction` and `locale` (`packages/stories/src/frame.tsx`).\n- **Portaled UI.** `mount` returns `#story-root`. Dialogs, menus and drawers render outside it, so locate them from `page` (`packages/app/component-tests/composer.spec.ts:101-107`).\n- **Server state** comes from the `resolve.alias` list in `packages/stories/vite.config.ts`, which points app server, SDK and sync modules at `packages/stories/mocks/app/**`. Extend a mock rather than adding a server. The app's components lab renders the same stories inside the real app instead, so a story must not depend on a mock-only export. Tests reset mock state on every mount.\n- **Requests the mocks do not answer.** Route them with `page.route`. That includes server endpoints such as `/api/fs/read` (`packages/session-ui/component-tests/read-image.spec.ts:20`) and external URLs such as favicons (`packages/session-ui/component-tests/markdown.spec.ts:217`).\n\n## Fixtures imported by URL\n\nUse these when the contract is not visible in a story, such as construction counts, unmount races or cache state.\n\n- **Fixture file.** Put a `*.fixture.tsx` file in `component-tests/` or beside the source it drives (`packages/gui-plugins/src/browser/panel.fixture.tsx`). It exports a `mountX()` that renders into a host with a `data-testid` (`packages/session-ui/component-tests/basic-tool.fixture.tsx:7-46`). Never end the name in `.spec` or `.test`.\n- **Mount it in three steps.**\n  1. Mount a light story first, so that the app styles load.\n  2. Import the fixture and call it inside `page.evaluate`.\n  3. Locate it with `page.getByTestId` (`packages/session-ui/component-tests/basic-tool.spec.ts:30-36`).\n\n  It renders outside the frame's decorators, so it must supply its own providers (`packages/app/component-tests/browser-pane-restore.spec.ts:69-83`).\n\n- **Module URLs.** Build each one with `sourceURL(new URL(\"<relative path with extension>\", import.meta.url))` (`story.ts:10`), written as a string literal in the spec (`browser-pane-restore.spec.ts:6-24`). Any other spelling either fails to load or loads a second module instance whose contexts the page never provides; a hand-written `/@fs//...` is the documented second-instance case (`story.ts:6-9`). The literal also matters for pre-bundling (next section).\n- **Aliases.** A module imported by URL is the real file. App code that reaches the same module through a `vite.config.ts` alias gets the mock (`browser-pane-restore.spec.ts:95-96`). Decide which of the two the contract needs.\n- **Spec imports.** Import source modules into the spec with `import type` only (`browser-pane-restore.spec.ts:2-4`). A value import runs browser code in the Node runner.\n- **Async order.** Let the test decide the order through deferreds the fixture exposes (`mountPluginHost`, `packages/app/component-tests/plugin-host.fixture.tsx:151`). Await the outcome they lead to (`plugin-host.spec.ts:38-69`), or poll in-page state with `until` (`plugin-host.fixture.tsx:141-148`). Do not copy the fixed in-page `setTimeout` waits that other cases in `plugin-host.spec.ts` (`:30`, `:80-82`, `:100-111`) use to prove a negative.\n\n## Vite pre-bundling and reloads\n\n`packages/stories/vite.config.ts` sets `optimizeDeps.entries`: every `*.stories.tsx` and `*.worker.ts` under the four `src/` trees, every module a component spec names by a `./` or `../` string literal ending in `.ts`/`.tsx` (collected at the top of the file), and the frame's `index.html`. Vite pre-bundles their bare imports before the first page loads. A dependency the scan misses is found on first import instead. If that changes chunks already served, Vite logs `✨ optimized dependencies changed. reloading` and sends `full-reload` with path `*` to every page in every worker, and requests with a stale `?v=` hash get a 504 \"Outdated Optimize Dep\".\n\n- **The bar is zero reloads.** A cold run of either suite should print neither `new dependencies optimized` nor `optimized dependencies changed`, so these lines are the signal to watch.\n- **Keep new code reachable by the scan.** A module path built at runtime, in a template literal, or without its extension is not collected, and neither is a spec named other than `*.spec.ts`. Name stories `*.stories.tsx`: the story index and the scan read only that extension. Name workers `*.worker.ts`, since the scan does not follow `new Worker(new URL(…))`. Add an `optimizeDeps.include` entry only for a dependency the scan cannot reach. Vite names it in `✨ new dependencies optimized: …`, and `DEBUG=vite:deps` replaces that line with scan and discovery detail.\n- **Symptoms.** \"page.evaluate: Execution context was destroyed\", \"Failed to fetch dynamically imported module …/@fs/…\", or fixture state that is gone right after `mount` (\"Registrations: 1\" not found, an empty tab strip).\n- **Only `mount()` survives a reload,** because it navigates back to the same URL. A reload loses everything set up after it: `page.evaluate` imports, mounted fixtures, state from earlier clicks, and the elements your locators pointed at. Another worker's discovery can trigger it at any moment.\n- **Fix reloads in the scan, never with retries, timeouts or import order.** The import-everything-before-mount `Promise.all` in `browser-pane-restore.spec.ts:43-58` (comment at `:56`) only narrowed the window. Before the entries existed, that file's `:123` and `:150` were retried in 31 CI jobs each, and failed attempts cost about 39-47 s (Linux) and 34 s (Windows) of worker time per run.\n- **Clear the cache whenever entries matter.** Vite's `getConfigHash` covers `optimizeDeps.include`, `resolve` and plugin names, but not `entries`, and a valid `_metadata.json` skips the scan. A warm cache therefore never scans a new story or fixture, so it shows neither the reloads nor the absence of reloads that CI's cold scan would. Delete `packages/stories/node_modules/.vite` before judging.\n- **Resolve through package resolution, not paths into `node_modules`.** `vite.config.ts` dedupes `@tanstack/solid-query` instead of aliasing it into `app/node_modules`, a path that exists only under bun's isolated linker. While Windows CI installed hoisted, that alias made every app story fail there, and 23 of 30 runs hit the step's 15-minute cap. CI now runs the same `bun install --frozen-lockfile` on every OS (`.github/actions/setup-bun/action.yml:66-68`), so such an alias would pass CI and still break a hoisted local install.\n\n## Cold vs warm, and what a mount costs\n\n- **CI is always cold.** Each suite runs in its own leg on a fresh runner (`check-test.yml:239-240`), so every leg starts the story server with no cache and runs the full scan.\n- **Do not count the first test.** In one CI run of this harness, the first test per worker took 5.7-7.0 s on Linux and 14-15 s on Windows. Warm tests had a median of 1.5 s (app) and 2.0 s (session-ui) on Linux and 2.4-2.6 s on Windows. A warm `mount` (navigation plus root wait) had a median of 1.0 s (app) and 1.5 s (session-ui) on Linux and 1.7-1.9 s on Windows, which is most of a typical test. Judge cost by warm attempts.\n- **Local reuse.** Playwright reuses whatever answers on the port (`config.ts:20`; default 6006, `config.ts:3`). That hides cold cost and reloads, and with several worktrees another checkout's story server can serve your tests its own code. Set `PLAYWRIGHT_STORIES_PORT` or `PLAYWRIGHT_STORIES_URL` (`config.ts:3-4`). On Windows pick a port below 49152: the dynamic range starts there, and Hyper-V and WSL reserve blocks inside it (`netsh int ipv4 show excludedportrange protocol=tcp`). Two story servers in one checkout share, and race on, `packages/stories/node_modules/.vite`, so a separate port is not enough: use a separate worktree.\n- **Keep mounts few.** Put one scenario in each test, and resize in place with `page.setViewportSize` (`composer.spec.ts:28-29`) instead of mounting again.\n  - CI records video only on the first retry, next to the trace; local runs keep `retain-on-failure` (`config.ts:27-30`).\n\n## Mobile, touch and drawers\n\n- **Touch.** Use `story.use({ hasTouch: true, isMobile: true, viewport: { width: 390, height: 844 } })` inside a `story.describe` (`packages/session-ui/component-tests/session-timeline-mobile.spec.ts:3-4`). These options are test-scoped, so the shared workers stay. Prove that emulation took effect with `matchMedia(\"(hover: none)\")` (`composer.spec.ts:244`), and interact with `tap()` or `page.touchscreen.tap` (`session-timeline-mobile.spec.ts:41-42`).\n- **No `launchOptions`.** They are worker-scoped, so the file gets its own worker hash and its own workers. App e2e had to move `packages/app/e2e/regression/mobile-timeline-scroll.spec.ts:18` into its own `fullyParallel` project (`packages/app/playwright.config.ts:69-86`) because it otherwise formed one long serial group running alone on a single worker.\n- **Drawers.** Below 768 px, pickers open in a bottom drawer (`composer.spec.ts:100-107`).\n  - @corvu/drawer 0.2.4 lost a close requested in the first frames after opening: focus stayed trapped in an off-screen sheet and `body` kept `pointer-events: none`. Before the fix, this caused most first-attempt failures of `composer.spec.ts:5`, in 17 of 31 Linux runs.\n  - `patches/@corvu%2Fdrawer@0.2.4.patch` (root `package.json` `patchedDependencies`) fixes it, so tests need no wait before pressing Escape or tapping outside.\n  - `composer.spec.ts:375-429` guards the patch. It dismisses in-page, before and as `data-opening` appears, because Playwright input cannot target those frames. Upgrading @corvu/drawer drops the patch, so rerun those tests.\n  - Before measuring or screenshotting a drawer, wait until it is fully in the viewport and has no `data-transitioning` (`packages/app/e2e/regression/mobile-model-pickers.spec.ts:25-26`).\n  - A stuck overlay is a product bug. Fix the product and guard it in-page, as the patch did, rather than waiting it out.\n\n## Geometry, platforms, known failures\n\n- **Fonts.** Inter's `@font-face` has no `font-display` (`packages/app/src/index.css:13-18`), so a story can lay out with fallback metrics and then shift. Windows CI saw 6.5 px in the composer overflow measurement (`composer.spec.ts:93-95`), which now waits first (`:25-26`). Before measuring, run `await page.evaluate(() => document.fonts.ready.then(() => undefined))` (`markdown.spec.ts:247`). The `.then` keeps the `FontFaceSet` from being returned to Node.\n- **Page scroll.** Outside fullscreen, the frame's wrapper is `calc(100vh - 2rem)` to fit the page's 1rem padding (`packages/stories/src/frame.tsx`). With `100vh`, every such story scrolled by 32 px, and any scroll-into-view shifted boxes (`composer.spec.ts:116` saw y go from 300 to 268). Do not give a story root `100vh`. A story taller than the viewport still scrolls, so measure relative to a container captured at the same moment, or use `parameters: { layout: \"fullscreen\" }`.\n- **Shortcuts.** Write them as `ControlOrMeta+…` (`browser-pane-restore.spec.ts:134`). Derive expected labels from `navigator.platform` (`packages/app/component-tests/session-question-dock.spec.ts:121`).\n- **Known local failure.** On macOS, `browser-pane-restore.spec.ts:123` fails after `ControlOrMeta+w` (Meta+W, `:134-135`), while CI (Control+W) passes. It is not your regression.\n- **`story.fail`.** It waits out the full 10 s expect timeout on every run: `packages/session-ui/component-tests/file-tool.spec.ts:49-50` took 12.6 s on Linux CI and 14.8 s on Windows CI. Fix the product instead, or make the expected failure an immediate check once the state has settled.\n\n## Before you push\n\nFrom the owning package (`packages/session-ui` or `packages/app`):\n\n1. **Repeat** at CI's worker count. Locally retries are already 0 (`config.ts:14`). Every attempt must pass: `bun run test:components -- component-tests/<file>.spec.ts --workers=2 --repeat-each=10`.\n2. **Read durations** in `bunx playwright show-report component-tests/playwright-report`. Compare warm tests with the CI medians above. Anything much slower needs a reason in the work the test does.\n3. **Rehearse load.** Late font swaps and focus restored after a menu closes (`composer.spec.ts:114-115`) race the test under load. In a throwaway copy of the spec, throttle before `mount` with `const cdp = await page.context().newCDPSession(page)` and then `await cdp.send(\"Emulation.setCPUThrottlingRate\", { rate: 4 })`. Alternatively, repeat while the other suite runs.\n4. **Run each suite cold** whenever you add a story, fixture, dependency, worker or lazy import, or change `packages/stories`. From the repo root, once per suite, as CI runs each in its own leg. Playwright pipes the story server's output into its own (`config.ts:22-23`), and a free port makes it start a fresh server:\n\n   ```sh\n   rm -rf packages/stories/node_modules/.vite\n   PLAYWRIGHT_STORIES_PORT=6216 bun run --cwd packages/app test:components -- --workers=2 2>&1 | tee /tmp/app-components.log\n   grep -E \"new dependencies optimized|optimized dependencies changed\" /tmp/app-components.log\n   ```\n\n   The `grep` must print nothing. Count optimizer events, not durations: under load, the same config ran anywhere from 127 to 189 s.\n\n5. **Windows.** Check platform keys and labels and font-dependent geometry. The Windows component legs are the authoritative check.\n   - Do not skip on Windows, and do not raise `config.ts` timeouts, retries or the `mount` wait.\n   - If one test does genuinely expensive work, call `story.setTimeout(...)` in that test, with a comment that names the work.\n6. **Confirm CI ran the component legs.** E2E never runs on pushes to dev (`check-test.yml:28`), so no post-merge run catches a regression.\n   - The `affected` gate filters on `@opencode/app` and `@opencode/stories` (`check-test.yml:60-61`). `ui`, `session-ui` and `gui-plugins` count through app's dependencies. `workflow_dispatch` forces the legs on (`check-test.yml:56-59`).\n   - `check-test.yml` is listed in `globalDependencies` (`turbo.json:8`), so editing it marks every package affected.\n   - The required `e2e (linux)` and `e2e (windows)` checks also pass when no leg was needed (`check-test.yml:329-352`). Look for `e2e (<os>, session-ui-components)` and `e2e (<os>, app-components)`.\n   - If CI skipped them, report your local cold run in the PR.\n\n## When CI flakes\n\n- **Download the artifact.** Run `gh run download <run-id> -n playwright-<linux|windows>-<session-ui|app>-components-<attempt> -D <dir>` (`check-test.yml:314-327`). Results are in `<dir>/session-ui/component-tests/` or `<dir>/app/component-tests/`. Only the first retry has a trace and, in CI, a video: `test-results/<test>-components-retry1/`.\n- **Find reloads.** The story server's output reaches the job log with a `[WebServer]` prefix, so grep the log for `optimized dependencies changed`. In the retry trace, a reload shows up as any of:\n  - a second frame (`/?id=…`) load and a second `[vite] connecting...`;\n  - the deps `?v=` hash changing between requests;\n  - 504 \"Outdated Optimize Dep\" on `deps/*.js`.",
  "opencode-effect": "# Effect\n\nThis codebase uses Effect for typed, composable TypeScript services, schemas, and workflows.\n\n## Source Of Truth\n\nUse the current Effect v4 source, not memory or older Effect v2/v3 examples.\n\n1. If `.opencode/references/effect` is missing, clone `https://github.com/Effect-TS/effect` there. Do this in the project, not in the skill folder.\n2. Search `.opencode/references/effect` for exact APIs, examples, tests, and naming patterns before answering or implementing Effect-specific code.\n3. Also inspect existing repo code for local house style before introducing new patterns.\n4. Prefer answers and implementations backed by specific source files or nearby repo examples.\n\n## Guidelines\n\n- Prefer current Effect v4 APIs and project-local patterns over old blog posts, examples, or package-memory guesses.\n- Use `Effect.gen(function* () { ... })` for multi-step workflows.\n- Use `Effect.fn(\"Name\")` or `Effect.fnUntraced(...)` for named effects when adding reusable service methods or important workflows.\n- Prefer Effect `Schema` for API and domain data shapes. Use branded schemas for IDs and `Schema.TaggedErrorClass` for typed domain errors when modeling new error surfaces.\n- Keep HTTP handlers thin: decode input, read request context, call services, and map transport errors. Put business rules in services.\n- In Effect service code, prefer Effect-aware platform abstractions and dependencies over ad hoc promises where the surrounding code already does so.\n- Keep layer composition explicit. Avoid broad hidden provisioning that makes missing dependencies hard to see.\n- In tests, prefer the repo's existing Effect test helpers and live tests for filesystem, git, child process, locks, or timing behavior.\n- Do not introduce `any`, non-null assertions, unchecked casts, or older Effect APIs just to satisfy types.\n- Do not answer from memory. Verify against `.opencode/references/effect` or nearby code first.\n\n## Testing Patterns\n\n- Use `testEffect(...)` from `packages/core/test/lib/effect.ts` for tests that exercise Effect services, layers, runtime context, scoped resources, or platform integrations.\n- Use `it.live(...)` for filesystem, git repositories, HTTP servers, sockets, child processes, locks, real time, and other live platform behavior.\n- Run tests from package directories such as `packages/core`; never run package tests from the repo root.\n- Prefer explicit test layers over ad hoc managed runtimes. Keep dependency provisioning visible in the test file.\n- Use scoped fixtures and finalizers for resources that must be cleaned up, including temporary directories, flags, databases, fibers, servers, and global state.",
  "opencode-opencode-dev": "# Debugging opencode itself\n\nWorkflow for interactively exercising the CLI/TUI and server while developing in this repo. All commands below run from `packages/cli` unless noted otherwise.\n\n## Server/client model\n\nopencode is a client/server system, not a single monolithic process:\n\n- **Server process** runs the Effect HTTP API (`packages/server`) and owns all domain state: sessions, database, plugins, permissions, Location services. It's started by the `serve` command (`packages/cli/src/commands/handlers/serve.ts`).\n- **TUI process** is a separate process that runs no application logic itself — it's an HTTP/SSE client of the server via `@opencode/client` (`OpenCode.make`).\n- **Discovery**: CLI processes find the shared server through a JSON registration file at `~/.local/state/opencode/service.json` (or `service-local.json` for the local/dev channel) containing `{id, version, url, pid, password}`; the password is used for HTTP Basic auth. Before reusing a registration, the client calls `GET /api/info` to confirm the server is alive, is the registered PID, and is version-compatible. `opencode service status` prints the registered URL.\n- **Sharing**: because of this registration/health-check dance, many concurrent `opencode`/TUI invocations converge on one shared background daemon rather than each spawning their own. If no compatible healthy daemon is found, a new one is spawned detached (`serve --service`) and registers itself.\n- **`bun dev service start|status|stop|restart`** manages this shared background daemon's lifecycle directly — useful when you need to force a fresh server, confirm one is running, or kill a stuck one.\n- **Standalone mode** (`--standalone`) opts a single invocation out of the shared daemon: it spawns a private one-off `serve --stdio --port 0` child tied to that invocation's lifetime, with its own random password. Use this to isolate a debugging session from your other running opencode sessions.\n- Every log line is tagged `role=server` or `role=cli` and a per-process `run=<id>`, so you can distinguish server-side and client-side activity in one shared log file (see \"Logs\" below) even when both roles are interleaved from concurrent processes.\n\n## Starting the dev TUI\n\n- `packages/cli` is the CLI and TUI entrypoint. `bun dev` from `packages/cli` and the repository-root `bun dev` both run `packages/cli/src/index.ts`.\n- `bun dev` connects to the shared local-channel background service (`service-local.json`), starting it if needed. Every worktree on the machine shares that service. Add `--standalone` for a private server tied to this one process.\n- Use `bun run dev:live` from the repository root to run this worktree's TUI against the installed `opencode` background server and your real sessions.\n\n## Interactive debugging with termctrl\n\n- Use `termctrl` for interactive checks instead of starting the TUI as a blocking foreground process. It provides a real PTY, handles OpenTUI's host handshake, and can save reviewable screenshots.\n- Use a dedicated session name and do not reuse or kill an unrelated session.\n\n```bash\ntermctrl start opencode-dev --host opentui --cols 112 --rows 34 -- bun dev\ntermctrl wait opencode-dev \"Ask anything\" --timeout 20000\ntermctrl show opencode-dev\n```\n\n- Wait for visible text before interacting instead of relying on fixed sleeps. Use the text expected from the screen under test, such as `Ask anything` or `Connect a provider`.\n- Drive the running TUI with `termctrl send`. Prefix typed input with `text:` and send control keys separately so the interaction matches real terminal input.\n\n```bash\ntermctrl send opencode-dev 'text:example prompt' enter\ntermctrl send opencode-dev ctrl-c\n```\n\n- Use `termctrl show` after each meaningful interaction and inspect the full visible screen for rendering errors, stale state, error toasts, and unexpected exits.\n- Save PNG evidence for every user-visible bug and fix. Do not save text captures; inspect the rendered PNG. Write temporary captures outside the repository unless the artifact is intended to be committed.\n\n```bash\ntermctrl save opencode-dev --format png --out /tmp/opencode/tui.png\n```\n\n- For resize-sensitive changes, resize the viewport, wait for the expected content, and capture the screen again:\n\n```bash\ntermctrl resize opencode-dev --cols 100 --rows 30\ntermctrl show opencode-dev\n```\n\n- Source changes may require restarting the process. Use `termctrl restart opencode-dev` rather than assuming the running TUI reloaded the change. Restarting the TUI does not restart the shared background service, so run `bun dev service restart` after server-side changes, or use `--standalone`.\n- To exercise background-service behavior, use `bun dev service start`, `bun dev service status`, and `bun dev service stop`.\n- Always clean up the Terminal Control session when the check is complete:\n\n```bash\ntermctrl stop opencode-dev\n```\n\n## Server/API debugging\n\n- Use `bun dev api --help` from `packages/cli` to inspect the API debugging command. It sends one request to the server using the same daemon discovery/auth path as the CLI.\n- Use `bun dev api` to introspect the server-side data backing the TUI. This is useful when debugging UI bugs: compare what the screen renders with the raw session, message, event, agent, or health data returned by the API to determine whether the bug is in the server state, the client data layer, or the TUI rendering.\n- `bun dev api` accepts either an OpenAPI operation ID or a raw HTTP method plus path:\n\n```bash\nbun dev api get /api/info\nbun dev api get /openapi.json\nbun dev api <operationId> --param key=value\n```\n\n- Pass JSON request bodies with `--data`/`-d`; the command sets `content-type: application/json` automatically unless you provide a header. Add extra headers with `--header`/`-H name:value`.\n- If no compatible background server is registered, `bun dev api` starts one through the daemon service. Use `bun dev service status`, `bun dev service restart`, and `bun dev service stop` when you need explicit lifecycle control.\n- Prefer raw method/path calls for quick server debugging and operation IDs when exercising documented OpenAPI routes with path or query parameters.\n\n## Auditing installed `opencode` sessions\n\nInstalled (released) sessions use `~/.local/share/opencode/opencode.db` and `~/.local/share/opencode/log/opencode.log`. A local/dev checkout uses `opencode-local.db` and `opencode-local.log` instead, and `OPENCODE_DB` overrides the database. Run `opencode service status` before `opencode api`, because `opencode api` starts a server when none is healthy.\n\nFor a supplied `ses_...` ID, compare three sources:\n\n- `opencode api get /api/session/active` and the Session/message endpoints for live server state.\n- The database's ordered `event` rows for durable history.\n- `packages/tui/src/context/data.tsx` and the relevant route for client projection and rendering.\n\nLocate an uncertain database without modifying it:\n\n```bash\nSESSION=ses_...\nfor db in ~/.local/share/opencode/*.db; do\n  sqlite3 \"file:$db?mode=ro\" \"select 1 from session where id='$SESSION' limit 1\" 2>/dev/null | grep -q 1 && printf '%s\\n' \"$db\"\ndone\n```\n\n## Logs\n\n- Log files live under `~/.local/share/opencode/log/`. In a local/dev checkout the active file is `opencode-local.log`; `opencode.log` is used for non-local (released) channel installs. Both are append-only, shared across every CLI and server process on the machine.\n- Each line is structured `key=value` text: `timestamp`, `level`, `run=<id>` (per-process run ID), `message`, and a `role=cli` or `role=server` tag. Use `run=` to isolate one process's activity and `role=` to separate client-side from server-side log lines, since a shared daemon interleaves many processes' output in one file.\n- Tail the live file while reproducing an issue instead of guessing from stale output:\n\n```bash\ntail -f ~/.local/share/opencode/log/opencode-local.log\n```\n\n- Filter to one run or role when the file is noisy:\n\n```bash\ngrep 'run=8fc3b1d5' ~/.local/share/opencode/log/opencode-local.log\ngrep 'role=server' ~/.local/share/opencode/log/opencode-local.log\n```\n\n- `OPENCODE_LOG_LEVEL` controls verbosity (default `INFO`); set it before starting `bun dev` or `serve` to get `DEBUG` output for a specific repro.\n- `OPENCODE_PRINT_LOGS=1` additionally tees log output to stderr of the process that emitted it, which is useful when a process fails before you'd think to check the shared log file.\n- `termctrl logs <session>` surfaces stdout/stderr for a Terminal Control session specifically (e.g. inspector output or startup failures before the TUI renderer starts) — use the log file above for anything emitted by a separate server/daemon process instead.\n\n## Heap snapshots\n\nOn every platform, including Windows, capture the running background server without restarting it or attaching an inspector:\n\n```bash\nopencode debug heap-dump\n```\n\nIt discovers the already-running service (never starts or replaces one), calls `POST /api/debug/heap-dump`, waits until the file is complete, and prints its server-local path. Pass `--server <url>` (with `OPENCODE_PASSWORD`) for a server started with `serve`, and `--directory <dir>` to write somewhere other than the server's log directory (the server creates it; a server that predates the option ignores it and the CLI warns). A 404 means the running server predates the endpoint.\n\nFor desktop memory, run **Capture heap snapshots** from the desktop command palette (`packages/gui-plugins/src/debug/main.ts`). It writes `~/Downloads/opencode-heap-<time>/` with the desktop's own (local) server, which writes straight into the folder, every app window renderer, and the Electron main process. Heap snapshots only hold GC-managed objects, so it also records Chromium's memory-infra dump (`memory-infra.json`, open in Perfetto) and summarizes it per process and allocator in `memory.json` (MiB: `malloc`, `partition_alloc`, `cc/tile_memory`, `gpu`, `v8`, …), next to each window's DOM size and decoded images and the server's `process.memoryUsage()`. `manifest.json` (PIDs, `app.getAppMetrics()` before and after) is written last. Start with `memory.json` when a process is larger than its heap snapshot. In a dev desktop build you can drive it over CDP (`OPENCODE_DESKTOP_REMOTE_DEBUGGING_PORT`, default 9222): press `Control+Shift+P`, type `heap snapshots`, press Enter.\n\nThe Unix `SIGUSR1` listener in `packages/cli/src/heap.ts` shares the same writer (`packages/util/src/heap-snapshot.ts`) and is still useful for a process that is not an HTTP server, such as a TUI client:\n\n1. Find the processes and inspect their roles and memory:\n\n```bash\npgrep -af opencode\nps -o pid,ppid,rss,vsz,lstart,etime,cmd -p <pid>,<pid>\n```\n\n2. Signal the process whose heap needs investigation. For shared-service memory, target the `opencode serve --service` process, not a TUI process:\n\n```bash\nkill -USR1 <server-pid>\n```\n\n3. Wait for `heap snapshot written` in the channel's log before opening the file. Snapshots are written to the same log directory as `heap-<pid>-<timestamp>.heapsnapshot`; writing a large heap can take several seconds and the file is incomplete until the completion message appears:\n\n```bash\ngrep 'heap snapshot' ~/.local/share/opencode/log/opencode.log | tail\nfind ~/.local/share/opencode/log -maxdepth 1 -name 'heap-<server-pid>-*.heapsnapshot' -printf '%T@ %s %p\\n' | sort -nr | head\n```\n\nUse `opencode-local.log` instead for a local/dev channel process. The log's `path=` field is authoritative.\n\n4. Analyze the snapshot with Chrome DevTools, a V8 heap snapshot parser, or a temporary tool installed outside the repository. Start with the largest retained objects, dominators, object counts grouped by constructor/name, and retainer paths back to GC roots. Relate suspicious names and paths back to the source tree rather than treating large shallow allocations as leaks.\n\nFor command-line analysis, install tooling under `/tmp/opencode`, not in the repository. For example, MemLab can rank dominators and trace a reported heap object ID back to a GC root:\n\n```bash\nnpm install --prefix /tmp/opencode/heap-analysis @memlab/cli\n/tmp/opencode/heap-analysis/node_modules/.bin/memlab analyze object-size --snapshot <snapshot>\n/tmp/opencode/heap-analysis/node_modules/.bin/memlab analyze shape --snapshot <snapshot>\n/tmp/opencode/heap-analysis/node_modules/.bin/memlab trace --snapshot <snapshot> --node-id=<id>\n```\n\nA single snapshot explains what retains memory at one point in time, but does not by itself prove a leak. For leak confirmation, capture a baseline, perform a controlled repeated workload, allow idle cleanup/GC when possible, capture another snapshot, and compare growth and retainer paths. Also compare snapshot heap size with process RSS: a large difference can indicate native allocations, database mappings, allocator fragmentation, or other memory outside the JavaScript heap.\n\n```bash\ncat /proc/<pid>/smaps_rollup\npmap -x <pid> | sort -k3 -nr | head -25\n```\n\nHeap serialization itself can temporarily increase RSS and allocator high-water marks, so record `ps`/`smaps_rollup` both before and after capture. Large anonymous mappings with a comparatively small live heap require native-allocation or allocator investigation; they cannot be explained from JavaScript retainer paths alone.\n\n## CPU profiles\n\nThe CLI installs a `SIGPROF` listener on non-Windows processes in `packages/cli/src/cpu-profile.ts`. One signal starts a ten-second CPU profile and stops it automatically; additional signals are ignored while a profile is active. There is no CPU profile CLI flag or environment variable.\n\n1. Get the PID from the info endpoint. For shared-service performance, target the server PID returned here rather than a TUI process:\n\n```bash\nopencode api get /api/info\n```\n\nUse `bun dev api get /api/info` instead when targeting the local/dev channel.\n\n2. Start the capture:\n\n```bash\nkill -PROF <server-pid>\n```\n\n3. Wait for `CPU profile written` in the channel's log before opening the file. Profiles are written to the same log directory as `cpu-<pid>-<timestamp>.cpuprofile`; the log's `path=` field is authoritative:\n\n```bash\ngrep 'CPU profile' ~/.local/share/opencode/log/opencode.log | tail\nfind ~/.local/share/opencode/log -maxdepth 1 -name 'cpu-<server-pid>-*.cpuprofile' -printf '%T@ %s %p\\n' | sort -nr | head\n```\n\nUse `opencode-local.log` for a local/dev process. Load the completed `.cpuprofile` in Chrome DevTools or another V8 CPU profile viewer and inspect the hottest functions, call stacks, and self time during the controlled workload.\n\n## Debugger\n\n- To debug the CLI or TUI with Bun's inspector, launch the CLI entrypoint through Terminal Control with an inspector URL, then attach a debugger to that URL:\n\n```bash\ntermctrl start opencode-debug --host opentui --cols 112 --rows 34 -- \\\n  bun run --inspect=ws://localhost:6499/ src/index.ts\n```\n\n- Use `--inspect-wait` or `--inspect-brk` when execution must pause until a debugger attaches.\n- Use `termctrl logs opencode-debug` for inspector output or startup failures emitted before the TUI renderer starts. Use `termctrl show` for the visible full-screen TUI.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Run `bun typecheck` from `packages/cli` after CLI adapter changes.\n- Run `bun typecheck` and `bun test` from `packages/tui` after shared TUI changes. Do not run tests from the repository root.\n- Treat automated checks and Terminal Control smoke tests as complementary. For user-visible changes, verify initial render, the changed interaction, Ctrl-C exit behavior, and save a screenshot of the corrected state.",
  "opencode-rtl-aware-development": "# RTL-Aware Development\n\nTreat direction as independent from language. Test English in both directions as well as real RTL and mixed-script content.\n\n## Guidelines\n\n- Set `lang` and `dir` on the document, and propagate direction through component providers used by portaled menus and popovers. Do not change the selected locale merely to force RTL.\n- Keep DOM and focus order semantic. Flexbox and Grid already follow `dir`; do not add `row-reverse`, CSS `order`, or reversed markup just to mirror a layout.\n- Prefer logical CSS for semantic layout. Reserve physical coordinates for pointer positions, canvas geometry, native window controls, and other genuinely physical placement.\n\n```css\n/* Avoid */\npadding-left: 12px;\nright: 0;\nborder-right: 1px solid;\ntext-align: left;\n\n/* Prefer */\npadding-inline-start: 12px;\ninset-inline-end: 0;\nborder-inline-end: 1px solid;\ntext-align: start;\n```\n\n- Isolate mixed-direction text. Use `dir=\"auto\"` or `<bdi>` for unknown text; keep code, URLs, IDs, and filesystem paths LTR without forcing the surrounding component LTR.\n\n```html\n<span class=\"file-row\"><bdi dir=\"auto\">README.md</bdi></span> <bdi dir=\"ltr\"><code>C:\\src\\app.ts</code></bdi>\n```\n\n- Mirror directional meaning, not every image. Back/forward, previous/next, disclosure, indentation, and directional progress may need mirroring. Do not mirror brands, clocks, media controls, charts, or text. Reverse physical gradients, `translateX`, SVG transforms, and animation deltas explicitly.\n- Map interactions through direction. `clientX` remains physical; resizing a logical edge needs an RTL-aware delta. Logical previous/next keyboard controls may swap ArrowLeft/ArrowRight. Follow the relevant WAI-ARIA widget pattern.\n- Do not assume LTR scrolling. RTL `scrollLeft` can start at `0` and become negative. Prefer `scrollIntoView({ inline: \"nearest\" })` or a tested direction-normalizing helper.\n- For Electron title bars, prefer native caption controls and use `titleBarOverlay` plus `env(titlebar-area-*)` for the safe content rectangle. Keep Windows/macOS native-control avoidance and `trafficLightPosition` physical; keep app navigation inside that rectangle logical. Mark interactive titlebar children `app-region: no-drag`.\n- Verify behavior, not screenshots alone. Check computed styles, pseudo-element geometry, hit zones, focus order, keyboard behavior, submenu direction, zoom/scaling, and both LTR and RTL scroll endpoints.\n\n## Test Matrix\n\n- English + LTR\n- English + forced RTL\n- A real RTL locale + RTL\n- Mixed RTL/LTR content, long labels, numbers, code, and paths\n- Keyboard, pointer resize, scrolling, menus/submenus, and Electron titlebar controls in both directions\n\n## References\n\n- [RTL Styling 101, Ahmad Shadeed](https://rtlstyling.com/posts/rtl-styling/)\n- [CSS-Tricks: RTL Styling 101](https://css-tricks.com/rtl-styling-101/)\n- [CSS-Tricks: CSS Logical Properties and Values](https://css-tricks.com/css-logical-properties-and-values/)\n- [W3C: Structural markup and right-to-left text](https://www.w3.org/International/questions/qa-html-dir)\n- [W3C: Inline bidirectional markup](https://www.w3.org/International/articles/inline-bidi-markup/)\n- [MDN: CSS logical properties and values](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Guides/Logical_properties_and_values)\n- [MDN: `dir`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Global_attributes/dir)\n- [MDN: `scrollLeft`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Element/scrollLeft)\n- [web.dev: Logical properties](https://web.dev/learn/css/logical-properties/)\n- [Electron: Custom title bar](https://www.electronjs.org/docs/latest/tutorial/custom-title-bar)\n- [WAI-ARIA: Window splitter pattern](https://www.w3.org/WAI/ARIA/apg/patterns/windowsplitter/)\n- [Kobalte: I18n Provider](https://kobalte.dev/docs/core/components/i18n-provider/)",
  "opencode-tui-tests": "# TUI tests\n\npackages/tui runs about 170 test files in one `bun test` process. CI runs it next to up to two other suites on 4-vCPU runners (`bun turbo test --affected --concurrency=3` in `.github/workflows/check-test.yml`), where it has taken 136-146s on Linux and 147-171s on Windows, most of it idle on frame pacing, production timers and sleeps. Most flakes come from waiting on the wrong signal and then running out of frames on a busy runner.\n\nRead the root `AGENTS.md` Testing section (no `globalThis` mocks, run from the package directory) and its TUI Stories section. To drive the real TUI, use the `opencode-dev` skill. For CLI subprocesses, service deadlines, Effect `TestClock` and turbo test tasks, use the `unit-tests` skill.\n\n## Frame budgets are not time\n\n- `waitForFrame`, `waitFor` and `flush` give up after `maxPasses` (default 20). `waitForVisualIdle` gives up after `maxFrames` (default 20). One pass is one rendered frame (`DEFAULT_MAX_PASSES` in `@opentui/core/testing`). \"Timed out waiting for frame predicate after 20 passes\" comes from this budget, not from bun's timeout.\n- **Started renderer** (`setup.renderer.start()`, as `createAppFixture` does at `packages/tui/test/fixture/app.ts:28`): frames tick at OpenTUI's default 30 fps, so 20 passes are about 0.66s. Production renders at `targetFps: 60` (`packages/tui/src/app.tsx:241`), so test frames are twice as slow as the real app.\n- **Not started** (the default for `testRender` and `createTestRenderer`): `waitFor` and `waitForFrame` fail within milliseconds when no render is scheduled, and the error shows `isRunning: false`. In the same state `flush()` and `waitForVisualIdle()` return immediately without error; they do not wait for fetches or timers. Await the signal, then `renderOnce()`.\n- Wait on frames only for something a frame produces. For anything else, wait on the signal itself:\n  - **Focus set on a timer.** `DialogSelect` focuses its filter with `setTimeout(..., 1)` (`packages/tui/src/ui/dialog-select.tsx:702`), which takes about 15ms on Windows. `typeText` sends keys to whatever is focused, so early keys are dropped and Enter then picks the default option. Put the focus in the predicate, as both dialog waits in `packages/tui/test/command-selection.test.tsx` do (`:68-70` for the model, `:75-80` for the variant): `setup.renderer.currentFocusedRenderable instanceof InputRenderable`. Before the variant wait had it, the test flaked in 5 CI runs in 20 hours, including a dev push, and a bigger budget did not help: it still failed with `maxPasses: 150`, because the keys were lost, not late. If a wait fails with the focus in the predicate, look for a state race, not the budget.\n  - **Markdown and code text.** `<markdown>` (`packages/tui/src/routes/session/message-parts.tsx:182`) draws no text until the shared tree-sitter worker replies, while frames keep ticking, so a plain `waitForFrame` can spend all 20 passes on a loaded runner with the text still pending. Use `waitForHighlightedFrame(setup, predicate, options?)` from `packages/tui/test/fixture/frame.ts`. When the predicate fails, it awaits every in-flight `CodeRenderable.highlightingDone` inside the frame predicate, and awaiting there does not use up passes. It accepts any setup with `renderer` and `waitForFrame`; app-lifecycle, session-group-navigation and session-home wait on transcript text this way. If the code under test accepts a client, inject `new MockTreeSitterClient({ autoResolveTimeout: 0 })` instead (`packages/tui/test/mini/scrollback.surface.test.ts:87`).\n  - **Requests and other state.** Await the deferred response your fetch handler returns. With no signal to await, poll with a wall-clock deadline instead of raising `maxPasses`: the file-local `waitForFrame(setup, matches)` at `command-selection.test.tsx:117` checks `captureCharFrame()` every 10ms for up to 2s. `wait` in `packages/tui/test/cli/tui/data.test.tsx:37` and `waitForModel` in `packages/tui/test/fixture/local.tsx:109` do the same for state.\n- `waitForVisualIdle` returns on the first frame with `cellsUpdated === 0` by default. A frame waiting on a highlight is also quiet, so wait for the text before you measure layout or assert on it: `session-home.test.tsx:227` waits for \"History message 0001\" with `waitForHighlightedFrame` after `waitForVisualIdle()` instead of reading the frame directly.\n- Settle steps cost frames. Each `waitForVisualIdle({ quietFrames: 3 })` takes at least 3 frames, about 100ms at 30 fps. `session-group-navigation.test.tsx` has 14 of them. When you know what you are waiting for, wait for that frame instead.\n- Do not use `maxPasses` as a clock. Several waits do, and each assumes 33ms frames:\n  - `app-lifecycle.test.tsx:792`: 120 passes to cover the 1s reconnect delay after `setup.events.disconnect()` at `:784`;\n  - `app-lifecycle.test.tsx:1721-1723`: 200 passes per retry-countdown second;\n  - `component/dialog-shell-output.test.tsx:144`: 100 passes for a 1s poll, plus `Bun.sleep(1100)` at `:163` and `:182`;\n  - `session-panel.test.tsx:590`: 120 passes in `ready()` for the initial load.\n\n  With test frame rates scaled 4x, dialog-shell-output (3 tests) and session-activity-anchor (2 tests) failed. The same budgets are why tests cannot yet run at production fps. A larger budget is fine only for a long run of frame-driven work, such as scrolling 50 rows.\n\n## Pick the renderer mode and clock\n\n- **Component logic:** `testRender(() => <X />, { width, height })` without `start()`. Change state, `await app.renderOnce()`, then assert on `captureCharFrame()`. One frame, deterministic.\n- **Time-driven renderables:** pass `clock: new ManualClock()`, call `renderer.pause()`, then advance the clock and `renderOnce()` (`fixture()` in `packages/tui/test/component/retry-provider.test.tsx:8`). `pause()` also stops `requestLive()` (spinners, animations) from starting the frame loop.\n- **Clock order:** inject a clock first (OpenTUI `ManualClock`; Effect `TestClock` for Effect code, see `unit-tests`). Use `jest.useFakeTimers()` only when nothing is injectable: it patches the global timers of a process that runs every tui file, so restore them with `jest.useRealTimers()` in `finally` (`packages/tui/test/util/delayed-presence.test.ts:24-26`).\n- **Whole app:** `createAppFixture` (`packages/tui/test/fixture/app.ts`) gives a started renderer, an in-process `Bun.serve` and `kittyKeyboard: true`. Call `await setup.ready` before the first wait. Passing `config` replaces the default `{ animations: false }`, so include `animations: false` yourself. The first use in a process pays `await import(\"../../src/app\")`: about 1s idle, 12-25s under heavy load. Use it only when the behavior spans routes or data.\n- **Server behavior:** use the existing `createFetch` and `createEventStream` fixture (`packages/tui/test/fixture/tui-client.ts:15`, `:69`) rather than spawning a server. The persistent PTY, for example, is faked as `/api/experimental/persistent-pty/...` routes plus a `Bun.serve` websocket (`packages/tui/test/session-wheel-focus.test.tsx:52`); real PTY behavior is tested in `packages/server/test/persistent-pty.test.ts`. Every fixture server listens on `port: 0` (`fixture/app.ts:32`) and is reached through `server.url`; never hard-code a port. Windows hands out ephemeral ports from 49152 up, so a fixed port in that range can already be taken by another connection.\n\n## Keyboard and mouse\n\n- Use `setup.mockInput`: `pressKey`, `typeText`, `pressEnter`, `pressEscape`, `pressArrow`, `pasteBracketedText`.\n- Create renderers with `kittyKeyboard: true`. Without it, `mockInput` silently sends something else (OpenTUI 0.5.17):\n\n  | Input                                        | Without kitty mode                        | With kitty mode |\n  | -------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------- | --------------- |\n  | `pressKey(\"u\", { ctrl: true, shift: true })` | `ctrl+u`                                  | `ctrl+shift+u`  |\n  | `pressKey(\"2\", { ctrl: true })`              | `2`                                       | `ctrl+2`        |\n  | `pressEnter({ shift: true })`                | `return`                                  | `shift+return`  |\n  | `pressEscape()` then `pressKey(\"a\")`         | `meta+a` (a lone Escape is held for 20ms) | `escape`, `a`   |\n\n- A second click on the same renderable within 500ms (renderer clock) and within 1 cell becomes a word or line selection. Do not sleep past that window: `Bun.sleep(550)` at `packages/tui/test/session-activity-anchor.test.tsx:120` costs 4.4s per file. Call `setup.renderer.clearSelection()` first, which resets the click history, or click at least 2 cells away. In a `ManualClock` renderer driven by `renderOnce()`, `clock.advance(501)` also works.\n\n## Terminal sizes\n\n- Always pass `width` and `height`. Otherwise the renderer uses `process.stdout.columns`: your terminal locally, 80 in CI.\n- Cover layout at a narrow and a wide width, for example `test.each([100, 44])` (`packages/tui/test/app-lifecycle.test.tsx:13`). After `setup.resize(w, h)`, `await setup.renderOnce()` or `flush()` before asserting. Run any real-time sequence at one width only.\n- Measure work by counting, not timing: mounted rows (`packages/tui/test/session-mount-budget.test.tsx`), rendered diff lines (`packages/tui/test/cli/tui/diff-viewer.test.tsx:2198`). `performance.now()` thresholds fail on slow Windows runners.\n\n## Timers, cleanup and shared state\n\n- A TUI test that takes over a second is usually waiting on a production constant; make it injectable instead of waiting it out. Two such constants: the shell output poll `setTimeout(poll, more ? 0 : 1_000)` (`packages/tui/src/component/dialog-shell-output.tsx:73`; 6 tests, 18.8s, 95% idle) and `reconnectDelay = 1_000` (`packages/client/src/solid/connection.ts:33`; about 1s per reconnect test). connection.ts already takes `idleTimeout` and `flushInterval` options, so add a sibling.\n- Clean up with `await using` or `finally`: destroy the renderer and stop the fixture server. Cancel timers first, or they throw \"TextBuffer is destroyed\" into the next test.\n- Remove the tmpdir last. Destroy the renderer and await the app task first, as `createAppFixture`'s `[Symbol.asyncDispose]` does (`fixture/app.ts:63`). Removing it under a pending write logs \"Failed to persist\" and flock \"lock is compromised\".\n- `TestTuiContexts` (`packages/tui/test/fixture/tui-environment.tsx:32-35`) defaults `home` and `state` to fixed `/tmp/opencode/...` paths. When the tree mounts a provider that writes under `paths.state` (`StorageProvider` in `src/context/storage.tsx:145`, `LocalProvider`, or the prompt history, frecency and stash providers), pass `paths={{ state, home }}` built from `tmpdir()` (`test/fixture/fixture.ts:8`). Process-shared paths are what keep tui from parallel workers: `--parallel=2 --no-isolate` halved local wall time (162.9s to 80.7s). Isolated mode re-imports `src/app` in each of the 18 test files that load it, at 6x the CPU.\n- Bun does not cancel a timed-out test body. It keeps running and can fail the next test or rerun as \"Unhandled error between tests\". Triage the first failure in a file; the ones after it may be leftovers.\n- Import another package's test helpers by relative path only from a package in tui's workspace dependency closure. `@opencode/tui#test` depends on `transit` in `turbo.json`, which folds tui's whole workspace dependency closure into the test hash, so `data.test.tsx:7` reaching `../../../../core/test` is covered because tui depends on `@opencode/core`. A package outside that closure is not hashed, and a change there can replay a stale pass (see `unit-tests`, Turbo test tasks).\n\n## Timeouts and failures\n\n- The tui and cli test scripts run `bun test --timeout 30000`; Bun's default is 5s. In tui, that budget exists for the cold `src/app` import, not for slow waits.\n- A frame-budget failure is not a timeout problem. Raising the test timeout does nothing for it, and raising `maxPasses` only hides the wrong wait.\n- A per-test timeout below 30s, like the 15000 at `session-group-navigation.test.tsx:249`, must still cover the cold import when the file runs alone. New per-test timeouts name their work in a comment (see `unit-tests`, Know the budget).\n- A frame-budget failure that reproduces on every run of one branch is a regression in that change, not a flake. dialog-shell-output failed \"after 100 passes\" in 21 of 29 tui runs on one PR stack and in 0 of about 102 elsewhere. Do not answer it with a bigger budget.\n- Do not add `skipIf(process.platform === \"win32\")` to hide a timing flake; fix the wait. Windows timers have about 15ms resolution, so a 1ms `setTimeout` race that never shows on macOS fails there.\n\n## Before you push\n\n- From `packages/tui`: `bun test --timeout 30000 test/<file>`. This matches CI's budget and prints per-test milliseconds, which the package script's `--only-failures` hides.\n- `bun test --timeout 30000 test/<file> --rerun-each 10`: all 10 must pass. The first iteration pays the cold import, so a timeout on iteration 1 under heavy load is that import, not your wait. Repeat while one other suite runs (for example `bun run test` in `packages/core`), not on an overloaded machine. Before its focus wait, the command-selection race failed 5 of 8 local runs under load.\n- Force the race you suspect: temporarily slow the timer or reply (for example the focus `setTimeout` to 150ms). The test must still pass. Revert afterwards.\n- Negative control: make the predicate impossible. The test must fail fast with a frame dump, not hang until the timeout.\n- Windows-sensitive behavior to check for: timers under 16ms, renaming a file another process holds, process kill, and paths shown in frames. On pushes and pull requests the unit job runs `--affected`, so `@opencode/tui:test` is scheduled only when tui, a workspace package it depends on, or a `globalDependencies` file in `turbo.json` changed. In the `unit (windows)` log, confirm the lines prefixed `@opencode/tui:test:` are there with `cache miss, executing` and a pass, and compare its `Ran N tests across M files. [Xs]` line with a recent run that included tui. Per-file timing is only available locally.",
  "opencode-unit-tests": "# Fast, Reliable Unit Tests\n\nCI runs `bun turbo test --affected --concurrency=3` on 4-vCPU Linux and Windows runners (unit job in `.github/workflows/check-test.yml`), so each suite shares the CPU with two others, and Windows is the slower leg. Most slow or flaky unit tests here wait on real time, spawn too many processes, or assert wall-clock speed.\n\nRead these first; this skill does not repeat them:\n\n- Root `AGENTS.md`: the Testing and Style Guide sections.\n- `packages/app/AGENTS.md` Tests (`packages/desktop` and `packages/gui-plugins` follow it): one owner per contract, and no production seams for tests.\n- `packages/ai/AGENTS.md`: cassettes. The `effect` skill: `it.effect` and `it.live`.\n\nSibling skills: `tui-tests` (OpenTUI renderer tests in tui and cli), `app-e2e-tests` (Playwright in `packages/app/e2e`), `component-tests` (Playwright on stories, `packages/stories`). This skill owns cli subprocess and service tests.\n\n## Know the budget\n\n- Bun's default per-test timeout is 5000ms. Check the package's `test` script:\n  - 30s: ai, cli, desktop, http-recorder, latex, merman, simulation, theme and tui pass `--timeout 30000`; core's `script/test.ts` passes `--timeout=30000`.\n  - 5s: client, httpapi-codegen and sdk pass `--timeout 5000`; app, codemode, function, gui-plugins, plugin, plugin-browser, script, server, session-ui, ui and util use the default.\n- Size Windows risk by counting spawns, not by local wall time.\n  - npm.test.ts \"installs a Git package from an npm ::path: subdirectory at a branch\" takes 0.4s locally and hit 5000ms on Windows CI. Each Npm.add Git case makes about 75 git process events: 3 `clone --recurse-submodules`, which run the git-submodule shell script.\n  - Assume roughly 40ms per Git-for-Windows spawn (estimated from an earlier git.test run on Windows CI: ~24s for 564 spawns). Some core file groups run 5-9x slower on Windows than locally.\n  - Those `test.each` cases (`npm.test.ts:223`, `:251`) hit 5000ms in 13 of 45 Windows core runs; that is why core's runner now defaults to 30s (comment at `packages/core/script/test.ts:38`).\n- A timeout loosens the budget only when it exceeds the package default.\n  - In a 5s package, work that legitimately needs longer gets a per-test timeout and a comment naming the work: `packages/server/test/fetch.test.ts:406-407` (\"Real retries wait 18 \\* 200 ms ...\", `{ timeout: 10_000 }`). `test.each(table)(name, fn, 30_000)` takes it as the third argument. Use file-wide `setDefaultTimeout` only when every test in the file does such work.\n  - In core, the four file-level `setDefaultTimeout(30_000)` calls only restate the runner default, and a per-test `{ timeout: 15_000 }` (still in `tool-shell.test.ts`, `session-generate.test.ts` and others) is stricter than it. Do not add values at or below 30s there.\n- Never absorb a sleep, poll or production interval with a larger timeout, `--retry`, or `skipIf(win32)`; remove the wait. Skip on Windows only when the platform lacks the capability, with a comment (`npm.test.ts:313`, symlinks).\n\n## Control time; do not wait for it\n\n- **Effect code.** `it.effect` from `packages/core/test/lib/effect.ts` runs on `TestClock`; `testEffect(layer)` adds your layer. server and cli import it from `../../core/test/lib/effect`; ai has `packages/ai/test/lib/effect.ts`.\n- **Fork, then adjust.** Fork with `Effect.forkChild({ startImmediately: true })` (`packages/ai/test/http-timeout.test.ts:58`), or await a Deferred the code completes and then `Effect.yieldNow` (`:80-81`). Otherwise `TestClock.adjust` can run before the sleep is registered, and the join hangs until Bun's timeout.\n- **Debounced streams and watchers.** `advance(condition)` and `drain` in `packages/core/test/lib/clock.ts` alternate one real 1ms macrotask with 500ms adjusts, because debounces register sleeps across fiber hops. Use `drain` (4 such steps) before a \"nothing happened\" assertion instead of `Bun.sleep(25)`. `advance` is bounded by 15s of real time, not a step count, because its condition can also wait on slow real work (a plugin import, a child, a large write) that took more than 100 steps on a loaded Linux runner (`supervisor-reload.test.ts` \"retains a discovered plugin change during initial activation\").\n- **Real I/O between virtual steps.** `TestClock.withLive(Effect.sleep(\"10 millis\"))`. `registered()` at `packages/cli/test/service.test.ts:740` tests the 5s registration watch with real file I/O under `it.effect`. This departs from the effect skill's \"filesystem → `it.live`\" on purpose: the timer is the contract.\n- **Real events need `it.live`.** Under `it.effect` the clock moves only when the test adjusts it, so an Effect timeout on a path driven by real processes, sockets or file events never fires and a missing event hangs instead of failing. The AppProcess tests (`packages/core/test/process/process.test.ts:13-14`) use `it.live` because the spawner's 1-second post-exit capture deadline (`packages/util/src/cross-spawn-spawner.ts:322`) never fired under TestClock.\n- **Give a racing component its own clock.** When a real-time deadline races slow setup, provide that component a `Clock` that only the test moves past the deadline. `packages/core/test/browser-idle.test.ts:24-38` wraps the live clock for `LocationActivity` (`:49-51`) and adds the TTL at `:84`, once the browser state is attached. Before, it waited out a real 2s TTL and failed on Windows CI with \"RPC is unavailable: experimental.browser\" when plugin activation was slow. Shortening a real TTL makes that race flakier.\n- **Product Effect code must stay on the caller's clock**, which is what makes both patterns above possible. Read time through `Clock`, wait with `Effect.sleep` or `Schedule`, and run nested effects with the caller's context.\n  - A bare `Effect.runPromise` runs on the default real clock. McpStdio does this (`packages/core/src/mcp/stdio.ts:66`, `:82`, `:109`), so `packages/core/test/mcp.test.ts:832` waits the real 2s `CLOSE_GRACE` on every OS.\n  - Capture `yield* Effect.context()` and use `Effect.runPromiseWith(context)`, as `packages/cli/src/acp/agent.ts:27` does.\n- **Code outside Effect** (app, desktop, ui, session-ui): `jest.useFakeTimers()` and `setSystemTime` from `bun:test`, restored in `afterEach` (`packages/desktop/src/main/plugin/lifecycle.test.ts:27-32`, `packages/desktop/src/main/storage/drafts.test.ts:15-17`).\n- **Advance fake timers to the next timer** (`jest.advanceTimersToNextTimer()`, `jest.runOnlyPendingTimers()`), not in small fixed steps. The 10ms-step `advance` at `lifecycle.test.ts:37` turns the 1,000-seed fuzz test (`:607`) into about 430k event-loop turns: 2.8-3.8s on Windows, 0.56s on Linux.\n\n## Real-time waits: deadlines, not attempt counts\n\n- Bound a real-time poll in milliseconds, generously, and return as soon as it holds: `until` at `packages/cli/test/service-lifecycle.test.ts:267` (30s), `eventually` at `packages/core/test/integration.test.ts:37` (10s).\n- Attempt-count loops hide a small budget that shrinks under load. Do not copy `waitUntil` at `packages/core/test/config/reload.test.ts:371` (100 × 10ms; the skills test at `:57` timed out after 1.2s on a Linux dev push) or `packages/core/test/plugin/provider-azure.test.ts:219` (3000 × 1ms).\n- Poll for every state you assert afterwards: the reload poll at `reload.test.ts:301-317` includes the skills that `:319-320` assert.\n- Let the test, not a timer, release a child's next step. The login command in `integration.test.ts:230-254` prints its secret only after the test, having seen the pending status, writes a release file; it used to print after a fixed `Bun.sleep(50)`.\n- Small timer gaps race too: `packages/core/test/process/process.test.ts:59-67` ordered stdout and stderr with 10ms and 20ms child timers and failed on Windows CI (`out 2` read before `err 1`); it now uses 100ms and 200ms.\n\n## When the wait happens in another process\n\nTestClock cannot reach a spawned server or worker. These production waits already cost real time:\n\n- cli (about 60s of its roughly 2-minute suite): the 15s bind-retry deadline (`Date.now() + 15_000` in `packages/cli/src/server-process.ts`), the 5s registration watch (`packages/cli/src/services/service-registration.ts:63`), and fixed sleeps (`Bun.sleep(8_000)` at `service.test.ts:620`, `Bun.sleep(5_500)` at `service-lifecycle.test.ts:237`).\n- core (about 8s locally): McpStdio `CLOSE_GRACE` 2s + `FORCE_KILL_AFTER` 2s (`stdio.ts:10-13`), Shell `forceKillAfter` 3s (`packages/core/src/shell.ts:306`, `:401`), and the 1s capture deadline in cross-spawn-spawner.\n\nTo avoid adding more:\n\n- Test the timing logic in-process under TestClock (the `registered()` tests at `service.test.ts:270-299`). Keep real processes for exit codes, signals, stdio and port binding, with one test per escalation path. Do not add tests that wait out these constants.\n- Shorten a production duration through an option, not by waiting it out. Precedent: real product options (`Flock.Options` in `packages/util/src/flock.ts:41-47`, `idleTimeout`/`flushInterval` in `packages/client/src/solid/connection.ts`) and client's hidden seam `withEnsureTiming` (`packages/client/src/service-timing.ts:31`, used by `packages/client/test/fixture/service-timing.ts`).\n  - For a spawned process the value must reach the child, as `timeoutMs` does through the worker payload at `packages/core/test/util/flock.test.ts:50-58`.\n  - server-process.ts has no override today, so its 15s tests cannot be shortened from the test side.\n  - app, desktop and gui-plugins forbid such seams (their AGENTS.md Tests sections); core, cli, util, client and tui have no such ban.\n- Retry timing belongs in core under TestClock. `packages/server/test/fetch.test.ts:363-408` spends about 5.8s of server's 16s in real 200ms bind retries (`packages/core/src/plugin/provider/openai.ts:21`). The fixed ports 1455/1457 are the contract there; new server tests cover routing and error mapping only. Elsewhere bind port 0 (`packages/app/src/runtime/server/global-sync/bootstrap.test.ts:81`).\n\n## Do not assert wall-clock speed\n\n- `expect(performance.now() - startedAt).toBeLessThan(500)` failed on Windows at 510ms (`packages/ui/src/context/marked-parser.test.ts:84`); `packages/core/test/shell-scan/adversarial.test.ts:77` has the same shape. merman's layout audit no longer asserts its elapsed time.\n- Count operations, or pick an input where a regression is catastrophic and assert only the result, so it trips the test timeout: `packages/codemode/test/openapi.test.ts:638` (depth 30 is 2^30 work without memoization). A ratio of two timings still fails on a load spike; if you keep one, use a generous factor and say it is timing-sensitive.\n\n## Spawn less\n\n- **Process start-up.** Each `bun run src/index.ts` CLI boot costs about 1.3s of CPU (the entry loads TUI `.tsx` through the preload in `packages/cli/bunfig.toml`). Test parsing and validation in-process; keep one or two real subprocess tests per file. Each cold bun child that imports the MCP SDK cost 1-3s; the MCP lifecycle tests, which spawned several, hit 5000ms on Windows until they moved to the in-process `resourceServer()` (`packages/core/test/mcp.test.ts:90`).\n- **Spawned CLIs** use `isolatedEnv(root)` from `packages/cli/test/fixture/environment.ts`: isolated HOME, XDG and database, shared transpiler cache. `packages/cli/test/upgrade.test.ts:98` and `debug-paths.test.ts:24` set a private `XDG_CACHE_HOME` without `BUN_RUNTIME_TRANSPILER_CACHE_PATH`, so every spawn transpiles again; do not copy them.\n- **Git.** Before the shared fixture below, a PATH shim counted about 2,850 git spawns per core run, and git.test took about 24s on Windows CI and 7s locally.\n  - Create repos with `packages/core/test/fixture/git.ts`. `initGit(directory, ...initArgs)` (`:24-27`) runs one `git init` and appends the fsmonitor, gpgsign and identity settings to `.git/config` in one write; `initRepo` (`:64-67`) adds an empty root commit (2 spawns; with one `git config` per key it took 6), and `gitRemote` builds on `initGit`.\n  - Do not write per-file `git init` plus `git config` copies. server still has two (`packages/server/test/vcs.test.ts:215-218`, `packages/server/test/worktree.test.ts:131-133`); server already imports core fixtures such as `tmpdir`.\n  - Use `-c user.name=… -c user.email=…` only for commits the test makes itself (`npm.test.ts:53`). Repos where production code commits later (snapshot, worktree, vcs) need repo-local identity.\n  - Keep identity repo-local. The unit job's \"Configure git identity\" step sets a global identity, but core's `script/test.ts` and cli's `isolatedEnv` replace HOME, so it is invisible there and visible to server and tui. That HOME isolation keeps core's missing-identity coverage: do not depend on CI's identity, and do not add a global gitconfig in core.\n- **Independent children** start together (`Promise.all`, `packages/ai/test/exports.test.ts:63`). `packages/app/test-browser/playwright-config.test.ts` runs sequential `Bun.spawnSync` (3.5s on Windows, 1.4s on Linux); `packages/desktop/script/windows-installer.test.ts` makes 6 sequential cold `pwsh` starts for ACL reads, which one `pwsh` call could batch.\n- **Size contention and thresholds to the claim.** `flock.test.ts:46` starts 16 bun workers; use only as many as still exceed the 4 CI vCPUs. A hard-coded threshold forces huge fixtures: `packages/core/test/git.test.ts:354` writes 2,100 files to cross `looseObjectLimit = 2048` (`packages/core/src/git.ts:50`), 2.7s locally and slower on Windows. Make the threshold an option with the production default, cross a small value in the test, and assert the default once.\n\n## Windows-only failures\n\n- Renaming onto a file another process holds open fails. cli `service.test.ts:462` failed on a dev push with `FileSystem.rename (...service-local.json.<uuid>.tmp)` from the temp-then-rename at `service-registration.ts:39`. Close handles the test holds; when two product processes race, fix the product, not the test.\n- SIGTERM cannot be caught: `process.kill` ends the target at once with exit code 1, so a shutdown handler runs only on POSIX. Assert per platform with `catchesSigterm`, `stopSignal` and `stoppedExitCode` (`packages/client/test/fixture/service-fixture.ts:7-11`).\n- Do not prove a stopped server released its port by binding it again. Windows hands out ephemeral ports from 49152 up, and any process, including suites running alongside, may take the port for an outgoing connection; a re-bind failed there with \"Failed to start server. Is port 65518 in use?\". Assert that nothing accepts connections (`expectNotListening`, `service-fixture.ts:108-117`).\n- `os.tmpdir()` reads `TEMP` on Windows and `TMPDIR` on POSIX; set both for a child (`packages/util/test/global.test.ts:22-24`). Compare canonical paths with `fs.realpathSync.native`, which expands 8.3 short names such as `RUNNER~1` (`:84-86`).\n- Reset a test socket once the request has arrived (`data`), not on `open`. Resetting on accept races the client's write, and on Windows the lost race is a write or connect error instead of a reset: `packages/util/src/retry.test.ts:25-30` recorded 2 of its 3 expected failures there. `packages/app/src/runtime/server/global-sync/bootstrap.test.ts:83-88` still resets on `open` and failed 3 of about 19 loaded local runs with `write EPIPE`, which `transientMessages` in `packages/util/src/retry.ts` does not list.\n\n## Temp dirs, files, process state\n\n- core's `script/test.ts` points HOME, XDG\\_\\* and TMPDIR (plus USERPROFILE, TMP and TEMP on Windows) at a fresh directory per run. Per-test dirs: `tmpdir()` (`await using`), `tmpdirScoped` or `withTempDir` from `packages/core/test/fixture/tmpdir.ts`.\n- Close watchers, databases and children before the dir is removed. On Windows an open handle fails removal with `EBUSY`; `remove` in `tmpdir.ts` then forces a GC and retries every 100ms, up to 30 times.\n- Restore process state: env with `withEnv` (`packages/core/test/fixture/env.ts`) in Effect tests and in `afterEach` elsewhere, real timers, and `setSystemTime()`.\n- `CI=true` in the unit job, and Turbo passes it through. Code that reads `CI` takes another path there, so a test of the non-CI path unsets it itself: `packages/http-recorder/test/websocket.test.ts:132` wraps its record-mode case in `withEnvironment(\"CI\", undefined, ...)`, because in CI that case replayed and waited forever for \"open\".\n\n## Keep files independent without `--isolate`\n\n- Except app, each suite runs all its files in one process with one module registry (core: 262 files), so state leaks between files.\n- Do not use `mock.module`; it leaks across files.\n  - It is why app's scripts pass `--isolate`: app unit went from 2.4s to 9.9s locally, and unit plus browser from 5.3s to 12-20s on CI.\n  - `packages/cli/test/mini-config.test.ts:25` and `packages/core/test/plugin/provider-gitlab.test.ts:74` already leak in non-isolated suites; do not copy them.\n  - In Effect packages, provide the dependency through a test layer. In app, desktop and gui-plugins, drive the real module and entry point, and inject only through a parameter the product already has.\n- Never add `--isolate` or bare `--parallel` (which implies `--isolate`) to a test script: ai went from 7.6s to 26.3s with `--parallel=2`.\n  - `--parallel=N --no-isolate` keeps the shared registry and measured 0.47-0.68x serial wall time at about the same CPU in core. It also exposed load-sensitive tests (browser-idle and integration's `eventually`, both since fixed).\n  - Adopting it is a runner decision for maintainers once CI passes on both OSes, not a fix for one slow test.\n- Runtime imports run in every file that loads the module. `packages/desktop/src/main/storage/store.ts:3` imports `electron` only for `getStore`, so `src/main/plugin/storage.test.ts` and `src/main/storage/store.test.ts` download the ~100MB Electron binary on a fresh install. On a first local run, storage.test.ts took 5.3s of file time for 0.1s of test bodies. Use `import type` for types (`packages/desktop/src/main/ipc-transport.test.ts:4`).\n\n## Turbo test tasks\n\n- `bun turbo test` runs every workspace package that has a `test` script, through the generic `test` task in `turbo.json` (`\"dependsOn\": [\"transit\"], \"outputs\": []`). A package with test files but no `test` script never runs in CI: give a new suite a script, and check with `bun turbo test --dry=json`. Add an `@opencode/<pkg>#test` entry only to override the generic task (desktop disables caching). Suites CI skipped rotted: client, util, sdk, http-recorder and merman needed fixes before CI could run them.\n- `transit` runs nothing and depends on `^transit`, so a test hash covers the package's whole workspace closure. The global dependencies add root `package.json` (whose `packageManager` pins Bun), `bunfig.toml`, `patches/**`, `.npmrc`, `.github/actions/setup-bun/**` and `check-test.yml`.\n- Tests do not wait for builds: workspace packages export their sources (`\"./*\": \"./src/*.ts\"` in `packages/core/package.json`), so a test must not read a dependency's `dist`. The build job's \"Build workspace libraries\" step checks the library builds on Linux.\n- A package that compiles `src` with tsc keeps colocated tests out of the build (`\"exclude\": [\"src/**/*.test.ts\"]` in `packages/util/tsconfig.build.json`). Otherwise `bun test` also runs the compiled copies, as Windows CI did with `packages/util/dist/retry.test.js`.\n\n## When CI flakes\n\n- Most suites print only failures (`--only-failures`): `(fail) <name> [5000ms]` and `^ this test timed out after 5000ms`.\n  - When `killed 1 dangling process` and `# Unhandled error between tests` follow (for example `NpmInstallFailedError` with `SIGTERM` from a `git clone`), the test was still doing real work, not hanging.\n  - The unhandled error is the abandoned Effect failing after Bun killed its child, not a second bug. Count the work (see Before you push) before choosing a fix.\n- Check whether the test also fails on dev pushes: `gh run list --workflow check-test.yml --branch dev --event push --status failure`. In one census before core's 30s default, 4 of 25 dev pushes went red, all on Windows unit tests (3 of them Npm.add 5s timeouts).\n- Read whole job logs with `gh run view --log --job <id>`; `--log-failed` omits green jobs, whose `Ran N tests across M files. [Xs]` lines you need for comparison.\n- Assert a child's stderr before its exit codes. `packages/core/test/util/effect-flock.test.ts:327-328` checks codes first, so its one CI failure never showed the cause.\n\n## Before you push\n\nRun from the package directory with the flags from its `test` script (app needs `--conditions` and `--preload`). In core use `bun run script/test.ts <file> …`, which forwards flags, isolates HOME and applies the 30s timeout.\n\n- [ ] `bun test <file> --rerun-each 10` passes every time. Add `--randomize` for order dependence; reproduce a failure with `--seed=<n>`.\n- [ ] Per-test durations: `--reporter=junit --reporter-outfile=<abs path>` (works through `script/test.ts`). Any test over about 1s does real work you can name.\n- [ ] Windows: count spawns. `GIT_TRACE=<abs file>` logs roughly one `trace: built-in:` or `trace: exec:` line per git process; a logging `git` shim first on PATH also works. At about 40ms per spawn, compare with the package timeout.\n- [ ] Rerun the file while the full package suite runs in another terminal; CI runs three suites on four vCPUs.\n- [ ] Grep the diff for `Bun.sleep(`, `setTimeout(`, `Date.now() +`, `performance.now()`, `Effect.runPromise(` in product code, `mock.module`, `git config`, and literal port numbers.\n- [ ] After CI: with `--only-failures`, the `unit (windows)` log hides passing durations. Compare your package's `Ran N tests across M files. [Xs]` line with a recent dev run's.\n- [ ] New package suite: `bun turbo test --dry=json` lists `@opencode/<pkg>#test`."
};
